SLIDE 1 — Title
Ride Optimisation
Minimising cabin seat RMS acceleration through intelligent suspension tuning

SLIDE 2 — The Problem
A 6x2 heavy commercial vehicle experiences continuous road surface excitations through its tyres — these vibrations propagate up through the suspension system into the cabin and are felt directly by the driver at the seat level.
The metric that captures this is RMS acceleration at the cabin seat — a standard measure of whole body vibration exposure defined under ISO 2631, directly linked to driver fatigue, discomfort, and long term health risk.
Current suspension parameters are designed conservatively around load and handling requirements. Ride comfort is secondary. Stiffness and damping values are set based on experience — there is no systematic, data-driven optimisation targeting what the driver actually feels.
The gap: No intelligent framework exists that models the full suspension dynamics and automatically finds the stiffness and damping combination that minimises cabin seat RMS acceleration.
This work addresses exactly that.

SLIDE 3 — System Definition: Physical Layout
The vehicle under study is a NVS 6x2 heavy commercial truck. The 6x2 configuration means six wheels total — two at the front steered axle and four at the rear tandem axle, of which only one rear axle is driven.
The suspension system connects three key masses:
	•	Sprung mass — the cabin and chassis assembly sitting above the suspension
	•	Unsprung mass — the axle and wheel assemblies
	•	Seat mass — the driver seat modelled as a separate mass connected to the cabin
Road input enters at the tyre contact patch and travels upward through this chain. The objective is to minimise what reaches the top — the seat.
(Include a schematic or free body diagram here showing the three masses and their connections)

SLIDE 4 — System Definition: Parameters
The key tunable parameters in this system are:
	•	Primary suspension stiffness (Ks) — spring connecting sprung mass to unsprung mass
	•	Primary suspension damping (Cs) — damper in parallel with the spring
	•	Seat suspension stiffness (Kse) — spring connecting seat to cabin
	•	Seat suspension damping (Cse) — damper at seat level
	•	Tyre stiffness (Kt) — road to unsprung mass connection, treated as fixed
Fixed parameters include sprung mass, unsprung mass, seat mass, and tyre stiffness. The optimisation variables are the stiffness and damping values — these are what we tune.

SLIDE 5 — System Definition: Assumptions and Boundary Conditions
To make the problem tractable the following assumptions are made:
	•	Road profile is modelled as a stochastic input using a Power Spectral Density function representative of a classified road surface
	•	Vehicle speed is fixed at a representative operating condition
	•	The system is treated as a vertical quarter car model — lateral and longitudinal dynamics are not considered
	•	Tyre is modelled as a linear spring with no damping
	•	Cabin and chassis are treated as a single lumped sprung mass
	•	The seat is modelled as a single degree of freedom subsystem on top of the sprung mass
These assumptions reduce the system to a well-defined, solvable ODE while preserving the dominant dynamics that govern ride comfort.

SLIDE 6 — ODE Formulation: Equations of Motion
From the physical model, applying Newton’s second law to each mass gives three coupled second order ODEs:
Seat mass:
Mse · ẍse = -Cse(ẋse - ẋs) - Kse(xse - xs)
Sprung mass:
Ms · ẍs = Cse(ẋse - ẋs) + Kse(xse - xs) - Cs(ẋs - ẋu) - Ks(xs - xu)
Unsprung mass:
Mu · ẍu = Cs(ẋs - ẋu) + Ks(xs - xu) - Kt(xu - zr)
Where zr is the road input. These three equations fully describe the vertical dynamics of the system. The objective — RMS of ẍse — is directly extracted from the seat mass equation.

SLIDE 7 — ODE Formulation: State Space Representation
The three second order ODEs are converted into a system of six first order ODEs by introducing state variables:
States: x1 = xse, x2 = ẋse, x3 = xs, x4 = ẋs, x5 = xu, x6 = ẋu
This gives a standard state space form:
ẋ = Ax + Bu
Where u is the road input zr. This form is what gets numerically integrated using a standard ODE solver. The state space representation also makes it straightforward to swap parameter values during optimisation — A and B are functions of Ks, Cs, Kse, Cse.

SLIDE 8 — ODE Formulation: Base Model Simulation and Validation
Before optimisation, the base model is simulated using nominal suspension parameters to establish a reference.
	•	Road input is generated as a random signal shaped by a PSD consistent with ISO 8608 road class
	•	The ODE system is integrated over a representative time window
	•	Seat acceleration time history is computed and RMS is extracted
This baseline RMS is the benchmark everything else is measured against. Any optimised solution must beat this number. The simulation also validates that the model behaves physically — natural frequencies, damping ratios, and time domain response are checked against expected ranges for this class of vehicle.

SLIDE 9 — Two-Approach Strategy: The Fork
Two parallel approaches are developed to solve this optimisation problem:
Physics-Based Approach
Built entirely from the ODE model. Every function evaluation during optimisation runs the full simulation. Accurate by construction — the physics is always satisfied. The cost is computational time per evaluation.
Surrogate Modelling — PINN
A Physics-Informed Neural Network trained to approximate the system response. Once trained, inference is near-instantaneous. The investment is upfront in data generation and training. The payoff is speed at deployment.
Why both?
Physics-based gives you ground truth and interpretability. Surrogate gives you speed and scalability. They are not competing — they are complementary. Physics-based validates, surrogate scales.

SLIDE 10 — Two-Approach Strategy: Pipeline Overview
(This slide is best as a diagram — two parallel flowcharts side by side)
Physics-Based Pipeline:
Road Input → ODE Simulation → RMS Extraction → Optimiser → Updated Parameters → Loop
Surrogate Pipeline:
Data Generation → PINN Training → Trained Model → Optimiser → Updated Parameters → Validate against Physics
Both pipelines share the same problem formulation — same design variables, same objective, same constraints. The difference is purely in how the objective function is evaluated.

SLIDE 11 — Nonlinear Damper: Physical Motivation
Real dampers are not linear. A linear damper assumes force is directly proportional to velocity at all times — this is a simplification that breaks down at high velocities and during compression vs rebound asymmetry.
A 2-stage nonlinear damper better represents real hardware:
	•	At low velocities the damper behaves softly — absorbing small road inputs without transmitting harshness
	•	At high velocities the damper stiffens — providing control during larger events
This two-stage character is what gives modern suspension dampers their ability to handle both comfort and stability simultaneously. Modelling this accurately means the optimised parameters are physically meaningful and implementable.

SLIDE 12 — Nonlinear Damper: Modelling the Force-Velocity Curve
The 2-stage nonlinear damper is modelled through a piecewise or smooth nonlinear force-velocity relationship:
	•	Stage 1 (low velocity): F = C1 · v — linear region with low damping coefficient
	•	Stage 2 (high velocity): F = C1 · v1 + C2 · (v - v1) — additional stiffening beyond a threshold velocity v1
The transition between stages can be modelled as a smooth continuous function to avoid discontinuities in the ODE integration.
(Include force-velocity curve plot here showing the two-stage character clearly)
This replaces the single linear damping coefficient Cs with a richer model that captures real damper behaviour.

SLIDE 13 — Nonlinear Damper: Option 1 vs Option 2
Two ways to incorporate the nonlinear damper into the optimisation:
Option 1 — Direct Nonlinear Optimisation
The nonlinear damper model is embedded directly into the ODE. The optimiser works on the nonlinear system from the start. The parameters it finds are directly for the nonlinear damper.
Option 2 — Linear Optimise then Fit Nonlinear
Optimise on the linear system first. Then fit a nonlinear curve to the optimal linear damping value to find the equivalent 2-stage parameters.
Both approaches produced similar RMS results. Option 1 is preferred because the optimiser is always working with the true system — there is no approximation step, the parameters are physically consistent, and there is no risk of the post-fit curve introducing error. It is a cleaner and more honest pipeline.

SLIDE 14 — Physics-Based Optimisation: Problem Formulation
The optimisation is formally stated as:
Minimise: RMS(ẍse) — seat acceleration
Design Variables: Ks, Cs, Kse, Cse — primary and seat suspension stiffness and damping
Subject to:
	•	Lower and upper bounds on each variable based on physically realisable hardware ranges
	•	Stability constraint — the system must remain stable under the given road input
	•	Ride-handling trade-off constraint in the multi-objective case
This formulation is the same for both single and multi-objective cases. The difference is in what the optimiser does with it.

SLIDE 15 — Physics-Based Optimisation: BayesOpt Single Objective
Bayesian Optimisation is chosen for the single objective case because:
	•	Each function evaluation is expensive — it runs a full ODE simulation
	•	BayesOpt is sample efficient — it builds a surrogate of the objective landscape and decides intelligently where to evaluate next
	•	It handles noisy, non-convex objective functions well
The algorithm builds a Gaussian Process model of the RMS landscape over the design space. An acquisition function — typically Expected Improvement — selects the next point to evaluate. Over iterations it converges to the optimum with far fewer evaluations than gradient-based or grid search methods.
(Show convergence plot — RMS vs number of iterations)

SLIDE 16 — Physics-Based Optimisation: pymoo Multi-Objective
The multi-objective formulation introduces a second objective alongside RMS acceleration — typically suspension deflection or dynamic tyre load, representing the handling and road holding trade-off.
pymoo is used with NSGA-II — a well established evolutionary algorithm for multi-objective problems. It evolves a population of solutions over generations, maintaining diversity and driving towards the Pareto front.
The result is not a single answer but a Pareto front — a set of optimal solutions where improving one objective necessarily worsens the other. This gives the engineer the trade-off curve and the ability to choose based on priority.
(Show Pareto front plot — RMS acceleration vs suspension deflection)

SLIDE 17 — Physics-Based Optimisation: Single vs Multi Objective Comparison



|         |Single Objective                 |Multi Objective                      |
|---------|---------------------------------|-------------------------------------|
|Output   |One optimal point                |Pareto front                         |
|Method   |BayesOpt                         |NSGA-II via pymoo                    |
|Objective|Minimise RMS only                |RMS + handling trade-off             |
|Use case |When comfort is the only priority|When trade-offs need to be understood|

Both converged to similar RMS values at the comfort-optimal end of the Pareto front — confirming consistency between the two formulations. The multi-objective result is richer and more useful for real engineering decisions where handling cannot be ignored entirely.

