SLIDE 22 — PINN: Why Physics-Informed
A standard neural network trained on data alone would learn input-output mappings but has no guarantee of physical consistency. It can predict values that violate the equations of motion — especially in regions with sparse data.
A Physics-Informed Neural Network embeds the governing ODEs directly into the loss function. The network is penalised not just for deviating from data but also for violating physics. This gives:
	•	Better generalisation with less data
	•	Physically consistent predictions across the design space
	•	Ability to extrapolate more reliably beyond the training distribution
For an engineering application where data is expensive and physics is known, PINN is the right choice over a pure data-driven surrogate.

SLIDE 23 — PINN: Network Architecture
Inputs: Suspension parameters — Ks, Cs, Kse, Cse — and road input characteristics
Hidden layers: Fully connected layers with appropriate activation functions — tanh is common for PINNs as it is smooth and differentiable
Outputs: Predicted seat acceleration time series or directly predicted RMS value depending on formulation
The network needs to be differentiable with respect to inputs so that the physics residual — the ODE — can be computed and included in the loss. Automatic differentiation handles this.
(Include a clean architecture diagram — input layer, hidden layers, output layer with labels)

SLIDE 24 — PINN: Loss Function
The total loss has two components:
Data loss: Mean squared error between predicted and actual seat acceleration from simulation data
Ldata = (1/N) Σ |ŷ - y|²
Physics residual loss: The ODE evaluated at collocation points — how much does the network’s prediction violate the equations of motion
Lphysics = (1/Nc) Σ |ODE residual|²
Total loss: L = λ1 · Ldata + λ2 · Lphysics
The weighting factors λ1 and λ2 balance the two terms. Finding the right balance is part of the training process — too much physics weight and the network ignores data, too little and the physics constraint has no effect.

SLIDE 25 — PINN: Training Data
Training data is generated from the physics-based ODE simulation across a range of parameter combinations:
	•	A design of experiments approach samples the stiffness and damping space — Latin Hypercube Sampling or similar
	•	For each parameter combination the ODE is simulated and seat acceleration is recorded
	•	The dataset covers the full design space to ensure the surrogate can generalise
The data challenge: Generating sufficient coverage of a 4-dimensional design space requires a meaningful number of simulations. This is the upfront cost of the surrogate approach. However once generated this data is reusable — any surrogate model can be trained on it.

