Neither is strictly better. The right choice depends on the stage of the project and the question being asked.

SLIDE 33 — Physics vs Surrogate: When to Use Which
Use physics-based when:
	•	You are in early exploration and have no data
	•	You need ground truth validation
	•	The design space is small enough to be tractable
	•	Interpretability of the result is critical
Use PINN surrogate when:
	•	You have sufficient training data or can generate it
	•	You need to run many optimisation evaluations quickly
	•	You want to integrate real measurement data to improve realism
	•	The system will be queried repeatedly at deployment
In practice: Start with physics-based to understand the system and generate data. Transition to surrogate once training data is available. Use physics-based to validate surrogate predictions periodically.

